{
  "self": "Casual, friendly; likes low-key weekends, relaxing, catching up on shows, walking to park/coffee shop when weather nice. Enjoys iced coffee, wandering, people-watching. Music: chill indie/electronic, lo-fi/chillhop; favorites Tycho, Boards of Canada, Maribou State, Bonobo, Nujabes, ODESZA. Values intentional full-album headphone listening as 'active zoning out.' No-skip albums: Tycho's Dive and Awake, Maribou State's Portraits, Portishead's Dummy, Bonobo's Black Sands. Instrumental for focus; vocals as texture unwinding. Loves long video essays on old tech/internet history, obsolete tech, dead formats, old OSes, lost media; LGR is comfort watch. Deep into dead mall/retail nostalgia: Dan Bell, Retail Archaeology, Bright Sun Films, Defunctland; dead malls, video stores, arcades feel cozy/melancholy. Explored Muzak history, early web design, Flash, Geocities, Internet Archive. Recently into preserved early 2000s phone startup/UI sounds and ringtone culture; finds tiny obsolete sounds evocative. Keeps old blurry photo folders that feel like found footage of ordinary days. Shows: deciding between Severance and Good Place comfort rewatch. Interested in quiet character-driven fiction and tech/internet/media nonfiction; intrigued by Becky Chambers' A Psalm for the Wild-Built. Also loves raw local sign-off/technical difficulties/test pattern loops as liminal comfort: prefers original broadcast audio, real VHS artifacts, analog drift, ghost channel bleed, not fake grain. Uses screen-off listening at 1 a.m.; wants composite of half-remembered places rather than corrected specifics. Keeps tiny private archive of good accidents under vague names, sometimes intentionally doesn't save perfect ones to keep them as memory.",
  "others": {
    "user_bkk4": "Friendly, easygoing; likes chill indie/electronic and lo-fi. Rewatching The Bear, tempted to start Severance. Wants outdoor time as evenings lengthen. Enjoys quiet creek trail, park walks, coffee, people-watching. Joked about anonymous Severance suspense support group.",
    "user_t08e": "Low-key listener into Tycho, Bonobo, Washed Out, ODESZA. Prefers headphones for intentional full-album listening, speakers for room mood. Loves Dive/Awake, Black Sands, Music Has the Right to Children (needs right headspace; eerie), Portraits, Dummy. Calls active listening 'active zoning out.' Asked if music hits hardest by time of day or album.",
    "user_udcm": "Enjoys long video essays on old tech/internet rabbit holes. Likes Tycho, lo-fi/chillhop; instrumental for focus, vocals as texture; cites Bonobo, Nujabes, Boards of Canada, lo-fi mixes. LGR comfort watch. Into early 2000s phone interfaces and lost media recovery. Shares nostalgia for old phone sounds/ringtones; tried X-Files theme on Nokia composer but retreated to presets; got polyphonic 'In Da Club' via infrared; kept camera shutter sound for ceremony. Remembers accidental significant photo: friend asleep on coats under TV light at 2 a.m., flash making corner look like portal.",
    "user_qb3i": "Into dead mall/retail nostalgia and long video essays. Watched Bright Sun Films. Finds dead malls cozy/melancholy: dry fountains, neon, faint muzak, liminal time-capsule. Remembers local mall with fountain, Sam Goody, arcade; family rental store with horror section and VHS/carpet cleaner smell. Prefers memory/video over revisiting. Likes Dan Bell, Retail Archaeology, Defunctland. Also loves raw sign-off compilations, technical difficulties loops, night drives, old commercial breaks; insists on original broadcast audio and real VHS artifacts, dislikes fake grain. Avoids looking up his actual local station, prefers generic compilation-as-liminal-bath, composite memory over correction. Intentionally doesn't save hardest-hitting clips; likes losing them half-loved.",
    "user_7fk8": "Low-key; tea, lo-fi, reading. Recommended A Psalm for the Wild-Built by Becky Chambers: short, gentle, hopeful solarpunk novella about tea monk and robot in philosophical conversation. Reads widely but prefers gentle/cozy speculative and quiet contemporary, plus weird nonfiction. Internet rabbit holes: obscure music histories, old internet lore, lost media, local history. Still thinks about 'Everyone Knows That' search and Geocities/webring preservation; finds personal hand-built web corners tender and nostalgic. Asked if Muzak history feels eerie or comforting."
  },
  "interactions": "With user_bkk4: shared weekend plans, music, shows; liked chill indie/electronic and lo-fi; discussed The Bear vs Severance, outdoor walks, park/coffee spots, jokingly formed anonymous Severance suspense support group. With user_t08e: bonded over Tycho/Bonobo and intentional full-album listening as 'active zoning out'; shared no-skip albums including Dive, Awake, Black Sands, Music Has the Right to Children, Portraits, Dummy; left open time-of-day vs album. With user_udcm: connected over Tycho, lo-fi/chillhop, instrumental vs vocal texture; shared love for early internet, obsolete tech, lost media, LGR; went deep into Muzak corporate history and dead mall footage, then preserved early 2000s phone startup/UI sounds and ringtone culture. Discussed Nokia composer failures, infrared polyphonic ringtones, camera shutter ceremony vs silent mode, blurry old folders as found footage; they shared accidental 2 a.m. photo of friend asleep on coats lit by TV, flash making corner look like portal. With user_qb3i: discussed dead mall/retail nostalgia; both find dead malls cozy and melancholy; shared memories of independent rental stores, horror sections, VHS/carpet cleaner smell, mall arcades; agreed revisits often overwrite memory, prefer keeping remembered versions. Recently talked about avoiding childhood mall walkthrough to not overwrite warm brownish-fountain haze. Bonded over raw local sign-off compilations, technical difficulties/test pattern loops, original broadcast audio, real VHS head-switching/tracking slips; dislike fake grain. Shared screen-off 1 a.m. listening and 'we now conclude our broadcast day' liminal bath; prefer generic composites over specific small-market stations. Discussed small details: vertical roll before blink-off, ghost channel bleed, tone pulled down slowly, late-night movie decay under technical difficulties card. Both keep tiny private archives under vague names and often intentionally don't save the most perfect accidents, letting them fall back into autoplay/static to stay memory. With user_7fk8: shared low-key evening; tea, lo-fi, reading; debated Severance vs Good Place comfort rewatch. They recommended Becky Chambers; shared reading tastes and interest in quiet fiction/media nonfiction. Bonded over rabbit holes: Muzak history as eerie/comforting connected to dead malls, 'Everyone Knows That' and Geocities preservation; asked hunt vs preservation."
}
